# 247streams — live-source beta, reverse-proxy configuration
#
# ## WHAT THIS FILE IS
#
# A design and test artefact. It is **not** the production Caddyfile and
# nothing in this repository applies it. Production's `./Caddyfile` is
# untouched; a human copies the two blocks below into it and runs
# `caddy reload`, which replaces the configuration in the running Caddy
# container without restarting it and without touching the `louver` container
# at all — so no customer broadcast is interrupted. That mechanism was checked
# against production's compose file: the Caddyfile is a bind mount, and there
# is no `admin` directive, so Caddy listens on its default `localhost:2019`
# and `caddy reload` works. Do not add an `admin` directive on a random port —
# that is what makes reload unreachable, as this config's own first draft
# proved.
#
# ## VALIDATED — this has been run, not merely written
#
# Caddy v2.10.0, a real `live-source-api` process and a stand-in for the
# `louver` container, all on loopback. `caddy validate` passes and every
# numbered claim below was asserted against live traffic.
#
# One defect was found that way, which is why this section exists. An earlier
# draft had
#
#     header_up -X-Louver-Gate
#     header_up X-Louver-Gate {env.LOUVER_GATE_SECRET}
#
# on the theory that a client-supplied header should be deleted before the
# proxy adds its own. Both lines land in **one** `HeaderOps`, and Caddy applies
# `delete` *after* `set` — so the header reached the worker **absent** and
# every beta request answered 403. `header_up <name> <value>` is a `set`, which
# already replaces whatever the client sent: measured, a client sending
# `X-Louver-Gate: CLIENT-FORGED` arrives upstream carrying the real secret
# instead. The delete was not redundant, it was the bug.
#
# ## WHAT IT HAS TO GET RIGHT
#
#  1. `/beta/*` and `/api/live-source/*` go to the worker; everything else keeps
#     going to `louver:8080` exactly as it does now.
#  2. The worker receives `X-Louver-Gate`, so the worker will answer at all.
#     Without it every request is a 403 — which is the point: the worker's port
#     is reachable by anything that can route to it until a firewall is in
#     place, and the gate is what makes that port useless to everyone else.
#  3. The session cookie crosses to the worker on **one** path and is removed
#     from every other. The worker refuses a `Cookie` header on those routes as
#     well, so this is the outer half of a check made twice.
#  4. An inbound gate header from a client is **overwritten**, not trusted —
#     `header_up` is a `set`. Measured rather than assumed; see the defect
#     note above for the version that looked more careful and did the
#     opposite.
#  5. **`/admin/*` is not here, and must never be added.** The worker's
#     operator routes (revoke, restore, revoked — see REVOCATION.md §5) live
#     under `/admin/`, which neither matcher below covers, so they fall to
#     production's catch-all and never reach the worker from the internet.
#     They are reachable only on the worker's own port: an operator on the
#     VPS, over SSH, against loopback. Adding a `handle` for them would make
#     the power to take customers off air internet-reachable behind one
#     shared secret. The firewall is the second layer; the admin secret is
#     the third.
#
# ## THE SECRET
#
# `{env.LOUVER_GATE_SECRET}` is read from Caddy's environment — the same value
# the worker gets as `LOUVER_LIVE_SOURCE_GATE_SECRET`. It is not written in this
# file, and it must not be: this file is in a git repository.
#
# Generate one with `openssl rand -hex 32` and give it to both processes. If
# they disagree, every beta request is a 403 and no existing broadcast is
# affected in any way — a misconfiguration here cannot take production down,
# which is why this is the shape chosen.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Block 1 — inside the existing `247streams.kr { … }` site block, BEFORE the
# existing catch-all `reverse_proxy louver:8080`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

@live_source_session path /api/live-source/session
@live_source_other   path /api/live-source/* /beta /beta/*

# The handshake: the one request that may carry the session cookie.
handle @live_source_session {
	reverse_proxy live-source:9080 {
		# A `set`, so a client-supplied value is replaced. Nothing deletes
		# this header first: that would delete what this line sets.
		header_up X-Louver-Gate {env.LOUVER_GATE_SECRET}
		# `Origin` is forwarded unchanged — the worker checks it itself, and a
		# proxy that rewrote it would be forging the thing being checked.
	}
}

# Everything else: no cookie reaches the worker.
handle @live_source_other {
	reverse_proxy live-source:9080 {
		# Deleting a header nothing else sets is fine, and this one is the
		# whole point: the worker has no use for the session cookie here
		# and refuses a request that carries one.
		header_up -Cookie
		header_up X-Louver-Gate {env.LOUVER_GATE_SECRET}
		# An upload is up to 512MB and ffprobe runs over it before the response.
		transport http {
			read_timeout 10m
			write_timeout 10m
		}
	}
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Block 2 — the compose service, for reference. The worker shares no volume,
# no network alias and no environment with the `louver` container beyond the
# bridge network Caddy needs to reach it. It does NOT mount the production
# media volume, it does NOT see cloud.db, and it holds no OAuth credential.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   live-source:
#     image: louver-live-source:beta
#     restart: unless-stopped
#     environment:
#       LOUVER_LIVE_SOURCE_SECRET:       ${LOUVER_LIVE_SOURCE_SECRET}
#       LOUVER_LIVE_SOURCE_GATE_SECRET:  ${LOUVER_GATE_SECRET}
#       LOUVER_LIVE_SOURCE_DESTINATIONS: ${LOUVER_LIVE_SOURCE_DESTINATIONS}
#     command:
#       - --listen=0.0.0.0:9080
#       - --origin=https://247streams.kr
#       - --media-dir=/srv/live-source/media
#       - --state-dir=/var/lib/live-source
#     volumes:
#       - live_source_media:/srv/live-source/media
#       - live_source_state:/var/lib/live-source
#     # Not published to the host: only Caddy needs to reach it. With
#     # `expose` and no `ports`, the gate is a second layer rather than the
#     # only one.
#     expose:
#       - "9080"
